@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE WAKE-UP CALL, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates, and the variable the page's `@if`
    binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it), which is what lets the markup move without a rewrite
    and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules:
    this card deliberately BORROWS the rescue offer's `.rescue-backdrop` / `.rescue-card` / `.rescue-hail`
    kit rather than inventing a z-band of its own, so its rules are RescueOfferCard's and stay where #1109
    filed them.

    DismissLoudPlanAlarm is wired STRAIGHT to `@onclick` and to the shell's OnClose, so it crosses as an
    EventCallback and the PAGE is the receiver of the press rather than this surface (#1135 clause 2).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* #952 — THE WAKE-UP CALL. Owner: "if we ruin it mid flight it would say so in the list and as a
       pop-up that we no longer have safely ending flight plan but will need input at some point. That
       nobody is flying the ship moment is important — say our pirate captain is asleep, there needs to be
       a visual wake-up call about the ship going off the plan." Fires ONCE on the valid → invalid
       transition (ArrivalStepRule.ShouldWarn owns that law and is unit-tested), never frame after frame,
       and re-arms only by the plan ending safely again. It reuses the rescue offer's modal shell so it
       lands in the established modal z-band rather than inventing another one. *@
    @* #989 folds the plan's SHAPE into the same wake-up call, through the same shell and the same
       one-shot law: a plan that cannot be flown as written (a cast off that is no longer the plan's first
       step, a second one behind it) is the same "nobody is flying the ship" moment as a plan that no
       longer ends safely, and a captain asleep at warp has no other way to learn it. One modal, two
       reasons — never a second pop-up inventing its own z-band. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="rescue-backdrop" @onclick="DismissLoudPlanAlarm">
            <OverlayShell class="rescue-card" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="btn btn-warning mt-2" DismissFace="I have the ship"
                          OnClose="DismissLoudPlanAlarm">
                <div class="rescue-hail">@LoudPlanAlarmHail</div>
                <div class="rescue-quote">@LoudPlanAlarmQuote</div>
                <div class="rescue-terms">
                    <div class="rescue-fee">@_planBreak</div>
                </div>
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}
